# --- site-factory generated artifacts ---
# Audit and outreach outputs are third-party business data, and CLAUDE.md
# forbids committing client data. The generic `out` and `dist` rules above
# already match some of these by accident; these entries are anchored to the
# repo root and state the intent explicitly, so the behaviour survives any
# future edit to the framework blocks above.
/audit/
/outreach/
# Ingested prospect data: their photos, logo, reviews, address and the
# generated config built from them. Same category as the two above.
/prospects/

# Real lead data, and everything else a run leaves beside it.
#
# `/data/*` rather than a rule per file extension. The previous version ignored
# `/data/*.csv` and `/data/usage-*.json` by name, which is the same mistake it
# was itself written to fix, one level up: `data/roster-50.json` — names, phones
# and place ids for 50 real businesses — matched neither pattern and showed as
# untracked rather than ignored, one `git add data/` away from being committed.
# See PR #54's Brief item 3 and issue #36.
#
# So: ignore the directory's contents, and re-admit the fixtures by name. A new
# artifact is ignored by default and someone has to argue it onto the allowlist,
# which is the direction the default should point. Anything re-admitted here
# must carry no client contact data and no secrets — that is the whole bar.
#
# Files this deliberately keeps out of git, all of them per-run rather than
# per-repo: the scored sweep CSVs, `roster-*.json`, `call-sheet.csv`,
# `known-prospects.txt`, `batch-log/`, and the `usage-*.json` Places ledgers
# (which carry no business data, but belong to a run — the one that matters is
# reconciled against the console and quoted in a PR, not committed).
/data/*
!/data/businesses.sample.csv
